---
description: Avoid hacky, bolted-on solutions when adding features
alwaysApply: true
---

# No Hacky Features

Don't tack features on with hacks. New functionality should fit the existing architecture, not work around it. A shortcut that "just works" but bypasses the design is technical debt we pay for later.

## Always do

- Add the feature where it belongs: the right module, service, or boundary.
- Extend or refactor existing abstractions when a feature doesn't fit cleanly, instead of routing around them.
- If the clean path is large, **stop and flag the trade-off** rather than silently shipping a hack.

## Never do

- Special-case flags, magic strings, or `if (feature === 'x')` branches sprinkled to force-fit behavior.
- Duplicate logic by copy-paste to avoid touching the shared path.
- Reach across layers to skip the proper boundary (e.g. a UI layer reaching directly into storage, bypassing the service boundary; reading global state to avoid passing a parameter).
- Leave a `// TODO: clean this up later` in place of doing it right when the right way is known and reasonable.

## Examples

```typescript
// ❌ BAD — bolt-on flag that special-cases one caller
function sendFrame(frame: Frame, opts?: { legacyHackForDeviceX?: boolean }) {
  if (opts?.legacyHackForDeviceX) { /* duplicated path */ }
}

// ✅ GOOD — model the real distinction in the type/abstraction
function sendFrame(frame: Frame, transport: Transport) {
  transport.send(frame);
}
```

## Allowed shortcuts

Intentional simplifications are fine when marked with a `ponytail:` comment naming the ceiling and upgrade path (see `external-rules/ponytail.mdc`). The line: a *scoped, documented* simplification is allowed; an *undocumented workaround that fights the architecture* is not.
